1. ${R4}/decisions.md (owner decisions, lead defaults, out of scope, hard gates).
2. ${R4}/plan.md: §2 principles, §4 glossary (one name per concept — authoritative for every name), §5 naming rules, §8 gates, tools and gate profiles, §9 hot spots and routing (and §6/§7 for the target trees and the public rename list when your package needs them).
3. Your work package file ${R4}/wp/<ID>.json: brief (authoritative scope and steps), files_owned, gate_profile, acceptance, risk.
Line numbers in briefs refer to 5fe22f4; earlier packages have moved code since, so locate items by name and verify every claim against the code (git log shows what earlier packages did).

LEAD NOTES FOR D17: Rust items moved or renamed since the brief was written; grep every Rust path and item that formal/ and docs/design/formal-proofs.md quote against the current tree and use the current names. Known stale quotes reported by the lead: formal/README.md:31 and formal/Formal/Harmonics.lean:6,:10, formal/Formal/SelectionRules.lean and formal/Golden.lean:6 (translation_plan.rs, harmonics(order), renamed in R36); formal/README.md:14 'illumination::Residual::pullback' (now cluster::IlluminateResidual::pullback in cluster/interaction.rs) and formal/Formal/ImplicitAdjoint.lean:7 'src/illumination.rs' (R40). The formal.yml path filters must list the final Rust file paths of every Lean-modelled item. Lean files: change only doc-comment lines naming Rust items.

HARD RULES:
- Edit only the package's files_owned. If a change outside them is unavoidable, make the minimal change only when it is a mechanical consequence of your package (e.g. an import path or a renamed call site that would otherwise not compile/run) and list every such file in open_issues; anything more goes to open_issues undone.
- Keep every feature, every test (moves/renames are fine with GATE-RT/GATE-IDS evidence), every #[inline]/#[inline(always)]/#[inline(never)] attribute, closure capture pattern, monomorphization and float-reduction order. Never loosen a tolerance, weaken a property or regenerate the parity baseline. Never touch benchmarks/results/**, parity, treams (Python original)**, the main checkout treams-rs, or other worktrees under scratch: lanes.
- Rust lints are strict (clippy all+pedantic+nursery -D warnings, missing_docs, unreachable_pub; edition 2024, toolchain 1.94); Python: ruff format + lint, strict pyrefly.
- Generated files (docs/api.md or docs/reference/python/**, llms.txt, generated regions) are regenerated with \`wt.sh docs\`, never hand-edited. CHANGELOG.md: append bullets at the end of the matching '## [Unreleased]' subsection only (merge=union).
- Lean-modelled functions (formal/: diffraction orders in geometry, lattice shells, translation selection rules, LU equilibration, the requested-illumination pullback) keep their semantics; cargo tests compare them with formal/golden files; keep formal/README.md names valid or update them if your package owns it.
- Comments and docs: present-tense rationale; no "previously/now/fixed/round/review/lane/revision/maintenance pass", no local paths, no process narration.
- WRITING STYLE (owner rule, decisions.md 'Writing style'): every user-facing text (docs, README, CHANGELOG, docstrings, rustdoc, comments, error messages) is simple, objective and approachable, in the spirit of Strunk's Elements of Style: omit needless words, active voice, positive form, concrete language with numbers and examples, short sentences. No meta commentary (nothing about the docs themselves, plans, reviews, packages, the code's history). No jargon: never write contract, gate, harness, lane, campaign, canonical, invariant (noun), semantics, surface, boundary (for an interface), leverage, utilize, facilitate; define unavoidable technical terms (pullback, cotangent, residual, Ewald split, ...) in plain words at first use. The plan's and briefs' wording is internal shorthand: never copy it into user-facing text.
`

const TOOLING = (w) => `
TOOLING — run from inside the worktree; every Bash call starts in a fresh shell, so always \`cd ${w} && ...\`. Use scratch: wt.sh for every build, test and gate (run \`scratch: wt.sh help\` or read the script for the full command list): build-ext, rust-test [filter], rust-lint, rust-fmt, rust-doc, rust-doc-private, rust-test-list, pytest <args>, pytest-fast, ids, py-fmt, py-lint, py-types, docs, docs-check, docs-build, docs-rust, docs-examples, lock, parity, bitwise [--battery PATH], uf, lat, hot, kit-copy, kit-diff <ID>, perf-ab <workload> [args], perf-cg <fwd|bwd> <workload> [args], perf-illum, gates, full.
- uv 0.12.3 lives at /usr/local/bin/uv (an older uv shadows it on the default PATH): run the lock command as \`PATH=/usr/local/bin:$PATH scratch: wt.sh lock [--check]\`.
- Never use just, uv run, uv sync, pip or maturin directly (they modify the shared venv or rebuild everything); \`wt.sh lock\` is the only way to touch uv.lock.
- cargo/pytest are pinned to CPUs 0-2; CPU 3 belongs to the perf station. The machine has 4 CPUs shared by three lanes: avoid needless rebuilds; build-ext reuses the merge-point extension automatically when your lane has not changed crates/.
- Performance: never build release in the lane. \`wt.sh perf-ab\` / \`perf-cg\` / \`perf-illum\` check out your committed HEAD in the perf station, build release there under a lock (expect waiting) and compare with the reference build treams (Python original): r4 (5fe22f4). Commit first, then measure.
- Bitwise gates: \`wt.sh bitwise\` (battery at 1 thread vs r4.npz, tolerance 0), \`wt.sh uf\` / \`lat\` / \`hot\` (ufunc/record/fast-path, lattice and hot-path dumps vs the r4 recordings through the gate kit's key/message map). If your package renames Python-visible or native names or changes messages: \`wt.sh kit-copy\`, adapt ONLY call sites/names (never inputs or keys) in the lane-local kit copy plus its map.json, run the gates with that copy (\`wt.sh bitwise --battery <copy>/battery.py\`, and the uf/lat/hot equivalents per the kit README), then \`wt.sh kit-diff <ID>\` to write the patch the lead folds in. Gate kit README: ${R4}/gates/current/README.md.
- GATE CACHE: wt.sh uf, lat and hot reuse the result wt.sh recorded for the same committed tree, extension and kit (a 'GATE-CACHE:' line says so). That is a genuine result of the same deterministic dump, not a skipped check. Use WT_NO_GATE_CACHE=1 only if you suspect the cache.
- PERF BUDGET (binding, decisions.md 'Performance-check budget'): profile 'move'/renames = perf-cg fwd once on <=3 kernels, NO perf-ab; 'refactor' = perf-cg on <=3 kernels + at most one perf-ab per workload (<=3 workloads), one rerun if >3%, then report to the lead; never write timing sweep scripts; reviewers reuse the implementer's numbers; <=30 min of perf-station time per package.
- Measurement caveats (from the tooling's calibration): perf-ab pair medians of the SAME commit vary up to ~4% while lanes compile on CPUs 0-2, so a single >3% A/B reading is not proof of a slowdown: rerun it, and treat it as real only if it repeats AND perf-cg shows an instruction-count increase on the same workload (otherwise report it for the lead's quiet-window re-measurement). perf-cg forward counts are exactly reproducible for the same commit; backward counts vary by ~0.1%. Run the battery and dumps only through wt.sh (it pins BLAS to one thread; under 3-CPU pinning a multi-threaded OpenBLAS changes last bits).
- GATE-RT (Rust test identity): \`wt.sh rust-test-list\` before your first change and at the end; same multiset of test names modulo the module-path map you document in the commit body. GATE-IDS (Python test identity): \`wt.sh ids\` before and after; identical modulo your documented rename map; skip count unchanged unless documented.
`

const COMMIT_RULES = `
COMMITS: small logical commits, each building and passing its focused tests. Conventional subject (feat|fix|perf|refactor|test|docs|build|chore: imperative summary <= 72 chars), a body explaining what and why (for moves: the old -> new path map; for renames: the old -> new name map; for test moves: the GATE-RT/GATE-IDS map), ending with exactly these two trailer lines (they replace the trailers quoted in decisions.md and the briefs):
${TRAILER}
Write multi-line messages to a file under scratch and use \`git commit -F <file>\`. Never amend/rebase/reset commits you did not create in this session, never push, never create branches. Finish with a clean \`git status\`.
`

